{"id":"gotcha-example-001","type":"gotcha","fact":"Example: Truthy check on numeric values fails for explicit zero - if (value) is false when value === 0.","recommendation":"Use !== undefined or !== null instead of truthy checks when the value could legitimately be 0 or empty string.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"coderabbit","reference":"PR review","date":"2026-01-09"}],"tags":["javascript","type-coercion","validation"],"affectedFiles":["**/*.ts"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-01-09T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-01-09T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"text":"metaswarm OOTB gap: /start-task does not gate on bd ready/bd doctor — when bd init fails (e.g., CGO-less host hits embedded Dolt error), the workflow proceeds silently with file-based fallback as if BEADS were healthy. Use bd init --server to bypass CGO requirement; tracked in metaswarm-dashboard#4 for upstream filing.","tags":["metaswarm","beads","ootb","silent-failure","cgo"],"discovered_at":"2026-05-07","severity":"high","applies_to":"any metaswarm-managed project"}
{"text":"Dead-KPI scorer: a rubric scorer can pass every synthetic-fixture test and still NEVER fire on real transcripts. The WU-4 thrashing scorer used a literal event-adjacency rule that real ~/.claude/projects transcripts never satisfy — caught only when the coder calibrated it against real data. ALWAYS validate a new rubric scorer against real transcripts, not just synthetic fixtures, before trusting its verdict.","tags":["rubric","scorer","calibration","sessions-spike-v4","testing","gotcha"],"discovered_at":"2026-05-18","severity":"high","applies_to":"metaswarm-dashboard"}
{"text":"eslint SPA write-guard: eslint.config.js has a no-restricted-syntax rule that blocks HTTP write-verb literals inside packages/web (enforces the SPA write-ban). A new SPA file that legitimately calls the dashboard's OWN write API (e.g. lib/ratings-api.ts) must be added to that rule's ignores list, or lint fails. Do not work around it by inlining the verb elsewhere.","tags":["eslint","spa","write-guard","sessions-spike-v4","gotcha"],"discovered_at":"2026-05-18","severity":"medium","applies_to":"metaswarm-dashboard"}
{"text":"Operator session ratings are stored at a DAY-INDEPENDENT path keyed only by sessionId: <dataDir>/projects/<name>/sessions/ratings/<sessionId>.rating.json — NOT date-partitioned. Rationale: a cross-day re-rate (operator rates a session, then revises the verdict days later) would split into two files under a date-keyed path and break the upsert/pre-populate flow. The session's own date lives inside the rating payload, not in the path. Caught by the plan-review gate.","tags":["rating-store","datalake","path","sessions-spike-v4","gotcha"],"discovered_at":"2026-05-18","severity":"medium","applies_to":"metaswarm-dashboard"}
{"text":"ToolUseEvent.summary is NOT a privacy boundary. It is truncated to <=200 chars but NOT redacted — it can quote operator secrets verbatim (a Bash command with a token, a file path). Safe on-machine (the datalake never leaves the host), but never export, transmit, vendor or share a SessionSnapshot or rubric evidence containing summary without first running a pattern redactor. Type-level hook: SessionSnapshot.persistedWithSanitization. Tracked in follow-up bead metaswarm-dashboard-5ui.","tags":["privacy","secrets","summary","sessions-spike-v4","security","gotcha"],"discovered_at":"2026-05-18","severity":"high","applies_to":"metaswarm-dashboard"}
